I solved the problem, only upgrading selinux-related rpm packages,
to default packages with --oldpackage option.

#rpm -Uvh --oldpackage selinux-policy-such...
I upgraded to old packages,
libselinux or checkpolicy policycoreutils and everything.

I could install my module in default F7 packages all right.

I'm calm now...

I will maintain my server under default module for a while.

In a sense, SELinux is good when you do not update.
Even if packages have security flaws, system itself is intact.



